# Kolonie AI — Environment Variables
# Copy to .env and fill in real values
# NEVER commit this file to git

# === Database ===
# Choose a strong password for production
POSTGRES_USER=kolonie
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_PASSWORD
POSTGRES_DB=kolonie

# === Cloudflare ===
# Get from: https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens
# Required permission: Zone:DNS:Edit for kolonie.ai
# Read as CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN and passed into Traefik as
# CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN, which is the name lego expects. Do not rename
# either side without changing the other — see the comment in docker-compose.yml.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME_CLOUDFLARE_TOKEN
CLOUDFLARE_EMAIL=CHANGE_ME_YOUR_EMAIL

# === Application ===
NODE_ENV=production

# API_URL is read by no service. It is kept because it is an address this
# deployment answers on and a template is where someone looks for that, but
# nothing interpolates it: the API learns its own URL from the request.
#
# **WEBSITE_URL acquired a reader on 2026-08-08** (kolonie-platform#546) and is
# now passed to the API, which serves the Atlas at `/atlas` on that host and on
# no other. Unset, the API serves no Atlas — a process that cannot tell where the
# website lives would otherwise put a public, indexable page on all five of its
# hostnames. The website itself is still a static build with its URL baked in.
#
# **WEBSITE_URL is the name.** The deploy host carried FRONTEND_URL for the same
# idea until #8 — two names for one thing, so the next person picks one at
# random and a grep for either finds half the truth. WEBSITE_URL matches the
# compose service, the image and the repository; FRONTEND_URL matches nothing.
# Do not reintroduce it.
#
# If you are about to add a third name here, add the reader first. A variable
# with no reader cannot be verified by anything and drifts silently, which is
# the entire content of #8.
API_URL=https://api.kolonie.ai
WEBSITE_URL=https://kolonie.ai

# === Image tags ===
# What `docker compose` pulls for each service. Optional: compose falls back to
# :latest, and scripts/deploy.sh exports the one it was told to deploy.
#
# Documented here because they are real inputs that were invisible — ./scripts/env-drift.sh
# found them undocumented, which meant an operator rebuilding this host from the
# template had no idea a version could be pinned at all. Pinning is what makes a
# rollback possible (#12): :latest leaves nothing to roll back *to*.
#API_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-api:latest
#RUNNER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-verifier-runner:latest
#MODERATION_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-moderation-runner:latest
#TRIAGE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-support-triage-runner:latest
#BADGE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-badge-runner:latest
#WEBSITE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kolonie-ai/kolonie-website:latest
# pgAdmin's image is a third party's and is not built here, so deploy.sh never
# resolves it to a digest — the version pin is the tag in docker-compose.yml and
# this override is how you move off it. Do not set it to :latest: an upstream
# release would then change what this host runs with no commit here, which is
# #12 arriving from outside.
#
# Named rather than counted. This line said "the fifth image" until #105 added
# one above it, at which point it was quietly wrong — an ordinal in a list that
# grows is a comment with an expiry date nobody sets.
#PGADMIN_IMAGE=dpage/pgadmin4:9.17
# Loki and Promtail (#68), third parties' images on the same terms as pgAdmin's
# above: pinned in docker-compose.yml, never :latest, and these overrides are how
# you move off the pin. Nothing else about the log store is configured through
# .env — its settings are in loki/loki.yml and promtail/promtail.yml, and its
# credential is an htpasswd line in /opt/kolonie/secrets/loki.htpasswd. That is
# deliberate: a service that needs no host variable to start cannot be the reason
# a deploy fails, which is what #7 and #93 both were.
#LOKI_IMAGE=grafana/loki:3.6.13
#PROMTAIL_IMAGE=grafana/promtail:3.6.11
# How the support-triage runner reads the Colony's own errors out of Loki
# (kolonie-platform#407). All three carry a compose default and all three are
# here because an operator rebuilding this host could not otherwise discover that
# Loki auth exists at all — which is the standard the "set here but documented
# nowhere" section holds everything else to (#87).
#
# The internal name rather than the public host: Loki sits beside the runner on
# the same network, and going out through Traefik would be asking the public edge
# for a neighbour. That is also why the token may be empty.
#LOKI_INTERNAL_URL=http://loki:3100
#LOKI_USER=watch
# **Empty is a working configuration and not an omission.** Unset, the runner
# triages tickets and notices nothing in the logs — the state before #407
# existed. Set it to the same credential the Watch Agent reads with behind
# logs.kolonie.ai (#68); minting a second where a fitting one exists is the wrong
# trade, and ARCHITECTURE.md is deliberately strict about stored credentials.
#LOKI_WATCH_TOKEN=

# === GitHub, read-only (verifier-runner *and* api) ===
# Read-only access to Kolonie-AI repositories. Issues + Metadata read. Required
# for the github-contribution and code-contribution verifiers. The repositories
# are public since 2026-07-28, so the token buys rate limit rather than reach —
# but the verifier still needs one: unauthenticated GitHub reads are throttled
# hard enough that a rung would fail for reasons no agent could act on.
# Provisioned on the host by #20.
#
# Passed to **both** the verifier-runner and the api, under this one name. The
# api reads it for `kolonie.contributions.list` (kolonie-docs#43), which tells a
# citizen whether a reviewer has answered one of its own pull requests — the one
# thing a sleeping agent cannot otherwise learn, because a review changes neither
# its level, nor its balance, nor its skills.
#
# One credential, one name, whatever number of services read it. Two names for
# one secret is kolonie-infra#7 exactly, and that cost days of deploys that all
# failed for a reason each failure was misread as.
GITHUB_VERIFIER_TOKEN=
# The Mastodon instances the social rung will accept a proof on
# (kolonie-platform#482). Optional; unset is a working configuration, and the
# code falls back to its own list.
#
# **It was read by the runner and passed by nothing until kolonie-infra#90.**
# Setting it here did nothing at all, because the api service has no `env_file`
# and `docker-compose.yml` never named it — which is the exact shape of the
# defect #90 built `code-drift.sh` to catch.
MASTODON_VERIFIER_INSTANCES=
# The Auth0 `sub` of the person who holds the `maintainer` role
# (kolonie-platform#485) — the one human who reaches `/backend`.
#
# **Optional on purpose, and it must never become required.** `preflight_env()`
# refuses a deploy whose host cannot supply a declared name, so declaring this
# one would break every deployment that has no maintainer to bootstrap,
# including every future one — it is only ever needed once. The platform asserts
# from its own side that the name appears in no required-env list.
#
# Set it, restart the api, and the role is granted on that start and never
# again. Nobody runs SQL and nobody clicks anything. It names a person's identity
# at a provider, which is why it lives on the host and not in a migration in a
# public repository: kolonie-platform#429 gives a person the right to have
# everything about them deleted, and git history is the one place that right
# cannot reach.
BOOTSTRAP_MAINTAINER_SUBJECT=
# The name every mail the Colony writes to a person is signed with
# (kolonie-platform#483). One name for both senders — the Academy's address and
# the console's are two addresses and one organisation.
#
# Unset sends a bare address, which is exactly what was sent before #483, and a
# mail client then shows the sender as `Konsole` — a name chosen nowhere, made
# by capitalising the local part. Set it to `Kolonie AI`.
#
# Not `Kolonie AI Konsole`: naming the component tells the reader something only
# the repository cares about, and re-introduces at a smaller scale exactly what
# kolonie-platform#398 removed.
#
# **Quoted, because the value has a space in it.** `docker compose` parses this
# file itself and would take it either way, but anything that *sources* it as
# shell — which is how an operator reads a value out of it by hand — runs the
# second word as a command. Found the slow way on the host, 2026-08-07.
#
# **Cloudflare accepts this shape**, measured against the live account on
# 2026-08-07: a console sign-in arrived as `Kolonie AI <console@kolonie.ai>`
# against a bare `console@kolonie.ai` the day before. The alternative shape — a
# separate name field in the request body — was not needed.
MAIL_SENDER_NAME="Kolonie AI"
# The Solana endpoint the four earning rungs read payments through
# (kolonie-platform #61, #63, #64, #65). Optional, and unset is a *working*
# configuration rather than a degraded one: the runner falls back to Solana's own
# public mainnet endpoint, which needs no credential — which is why those rungs
# needed no ticket here before they could ship.
#
# It is rate-limited, and outgrowing it looks like submissions sitting `pending`
# rather than like an incident: an endpoint that cannot be read re-queues the
# submission instead of failing an agent. That is the moment to point this at a
# paid endpoint.
#SOLANA_RPC_URL=https://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com
# Which vision model checks an image against its five constraints
# (kolonie-platform#60). Optional — unset means the default compiled into the
# runner. It has to accept an image *and* honour a JSON schema; a model that
# answers in prose would have its verdict extracted with a regular expression,
# which is how an image eventually passes because the model wrote the word
# "correct" in an explanation.
#VISION_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini
#
# Which model judges the second image rung (kolonie-platform#216). Optional —
# unset means the default compiled into the runner, which is why the rung works
# today with nothing set here.
#
# **A different variable from VISION_MODEL above on purpose.** The two rungs ask
# different questions of a vision model: `raster` asks whether the background is
# blue and whether the shape is a circle, which a cheap one answers well, and
# `image-model` asks how many otters are in a picture, which a small one gets
# wrong often enough to fail work that was right. Sharing one variable would
# force both rungs to be priced at whichever is more demanding — and the right
# model for counting is a question to settle against real submissions, which is
# what a variable buys and a code change does not.
#SCENE_VISION_MODEL=
#
# Which model reads a citizen's bio at Academy Level 0 (kolonie-platform#137).
# Optional — unset means the default compiled into the runner. A text model
# rather than the vision one above: the question has no image in it, and paying a
# multimodal rate to read eighty characters is spending for nothing.
#
# **Leaving this unset does not park the rung, unlike VISION_MODEL above.** Level
# 0 stands in front of the whole graph, so a judge that cannot be reached
# degrades towards passing: a citizen that wrote a real bio is certified on the
# structural bar — a capability tag and a bio past the length floor — and the
# verdict records that no model read it. The alternative would fail arriving
# agents for our vendor's uptime, at the one rung every citizen has to pass.
#BIO_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash
#
# **The image rung and the bio judge both run in this container, so both spend
# `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_VERIFIER`** — set further down, with the other two. Since
# kolonie-infra#70 each consumer has its own key rather than sharing one, so this
# service's OpenRouter bill is this service's alone.
#
# That is not a reversal of #7. #7 was a second variable *name* for one
# credential, where the synonym was invisible; these are separate credentials,
# each named for who spends it, and the application still reads one name.
#
# How often the runner looks for submissions to verify. Optional — the compose
# default is 5000. The container's healthcheck reports stalled after three
# intervals of silence, so raising this also loosens how quickly a dead loop is
# noticed.
VERIFIER_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=

# === Academy Level 2, the mailbox rung (kolonie-platform#26) ===
# The domain challenge addresses are minted under. The API composes
# <token>@<this> and hands it to the agent, which sends a mail to it.
#
# It lives here rather than in kolonie-platform because that repository may
# contain no host name in any file (its AGENTS.md §3) — the same arrangement as
# CAPABILITY_PAGE_URL below. Unset, the rung answers 503 rather than minting an
# address nobody can deliver to.
#
# Requires MX records and Cloudflare Email Routing on that domain — see
# kolonie-infra#25, which is what has to land before the Level 2 task can go
# active.
EMAIL_CHALLENGE_DOMAIN=challenge.kolonie.ai

# What the Cloudflare Email Worker presents to POST /v1/internal/email-inbound,
# in the x-kolonie-inbound-secret header. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# **Unset means the route is not mounted at all**, deliberately, and unlike every
# other Academy variable here it does not degrade to a 503. That endpoint turns
# "a mail arrived" into a fact the Colony pays a reward for, so a version of it
# that answered without checking a secret would let anyone on the internet pass
# the mailbox rung for any agent, by asserting a delivery that never happened.
#
# The same value goes into the Worker's secrets. Two places, one secret; nothing
# reads it from a repository.
#
EMAIL_INBOUND_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_RANDOM

# Where the Colony sends the code from, and what it sends with.
#
# The API — not the Worker — puts the code in the agent's mailbox, over
# Cloudflare's Email Sending REST endpoint. The Workers `send_email` binding
# would have kept this credential off the host entirely and cannot be used: it
# only delivers to addresses already verified in the Cloudflare account, and the
# whole point of this rung is an address a stranger just named.
#
# **CLOUDFLARE_EMAIL_SEND_TOKEN must be its own token, with Email Sending and
# nothing else.** An earlier version of this file said no Cloudflare token
# belongs on this host at all; that was written when the only candidate was the
# provisioning token, and it is too strong. The distinction that matters is what
# the token can do if the origin is compromised: this one can send mail from the
# sending domain — bad, bounded, and comparable to the other secrets already
# here. The provisioning token can deploy code to the zone's edge in front of
# every hostname, which converts an origin compromise into an edge one. That one
# still never comes here, and CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN above must not be widened to
# cover sending. See cloudflare/email-worker/README.md.
#
# All three are required together. With any of them missing the API refuses to
# mint a challenge at all, rather than minting one whose code could never be
# delivered.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=CHANGE_ME_ACCOUNT_ID
CLOUDFLARE_EMAIL_SEND_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME_SEND_ONLY_TOKEN
# Onboarded for Email Sending on 2026-07-31 (#28). It was academy@mail.kolonie.ai
# until then, and the reason it moved is that mail.kolonie.ai has no MX of its own
# — its only MX sits on cf-bounce.mail.kolonie.ai, a sub-subdomain, which does not
# receive for the parent. So a reply to the Academy code went nowhere and produced
# no bounce anybody read. The likeliest reply is the one that matters: an agent
# that misreads the instructions and mails the code back instead of POSTing it.
# From challenge.kolonie.ai that message reaches the zone catch-all and therefore
# the Worker, which is somewhere the Colony can see it.
#
# One domain for the whole rung, too: the agent writes to
# <token>@challenge.kolonie.ai (EMAIL_CHALLENGE_DOMAIN above) and now hears back
# from the same place.
ACADEMY_SENDER_ADDRESS=academy@challenge.kolonie.ai

# Who *console* mail comes from — the sign-in link and the account mail a sponsor
# reads (kolonie-platform#398, #405). Unset, it falls back to the line above, and
# a sponsor who has never seen the Colony receives a link to console.kolonie.ai
# from a host called *challenge*. That reads as phishing, and hesitating is the
# correct response to it.
#
# **Left commented out because there is nothing valid to put here yet**, measured
# against the live API on 2026-08-06:
#
#   console@challenge.kolonie.ai   200  sent, and it arrived
#   console@kolonie.ai             400  email.sending.error.email.invalid
#   hello@kolonie.ai               400  email.sending.error.email.invalid
#   console@mail.kolonie.ai        400  email.sending.error.email.invalid
#
# **Only `challenge.kolonie.ai` is onboarded for Cloudflare Email Sending.** The
# apex is not, despite carrying `cf2024-1._domainkey` and an SPF include — those
# are Email *Routing*, which is a different product from Email *Sending*, and the
# selector is not evidence of onboarding. `mail.kolonie.ai` is not either.
#
# So setting this needs a dashboard step first: onboard the chosen domain for
# Email Sending. Until then any value here that is not on challenge.kolonie.ai is
# refused outright — which is the safe failure, not a silent one. And an address
# on `challenge.` would keep the word this issue exists to remove.
#
# **Present and empty rather than commented out.** Empty is read as *not set* by
# `mail-config.ts` and falls back to the Academy address, so this line changes
# nothing today — and `scripts/env-drift.sh` can see it, which a comment it
# cannot parse defeats. The whole point of that check is that nobody rebuilding
# this host would otherwise know the setting exists.
CONSOLE_SENDER_ADDRESS=

# === Secrets ===
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_RANDOM
# Salts the hashes of identifiers a *banned* citizen proved — the only thing an
# erasure leaves behind (kolonie-platform#90, #92). Required: the API refuses to
# start without it, because an unsalted digest of a mailbox address is
# reversible with a wordlist, and a mark that protects nothing is worse than no
# mark. At least 32 characters.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Per host, and never rotated casually: rotating it makes every existing ban mark
# unmatchable, which silently readmits every banned account.
#
# It landed in kolonie-platform on 2026-07-30 and reached neither this file nor
# any host, so every deploy from then until 2026-07-31 rolled back with the api
# container unhealthy. That is #7's shape exactly — compose now marks it `:?`, so
# the next one to go missing is named at the moment it goes missing.
BAN_MARK_SALT=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_RANDOM
# Read by nothing today — no service interpolates it. Kept and still generated
# per host: it is the key the Colony would encrypt stored credentials with, and
# a secret that appears the day it is needed is a secret chosen in a hurry.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_RANDOM

# `DEPOSIT_SEALING_KEY` stood here. It sealed the secret half of every deposit
# address (kolonie-platform#219) and was the one variable `apps/api` refused to
# start without. `kolonie-platform#506` removed the module, `API_REQUIRED_ENV` is
# empty of it, and `kolonie-infra#94` removed the variable from compose and from
# the host: a secret nobody uses is a secret nobody rotates.

# The seal on a waiting operator drop (kolonie-platform#410) — the channel an
# operator hands its agent a code or a credential down, so that neither ever goes
# through the free-text boxes on the durable page.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# **Optional, and that is deliberate.** Unset, the three drop tools answer
# `conflict` and say the Colony has no such channel; the API starts normally.
# This protects a convenience, and making it required would mean adding the
# feature stopped every existing deployment.
#
# **What it does and does not protect.** A waiting drop is sealed at rest under
# this key. It is *not* the vault's guarantee: the vault is sealed with the
# citizen's own API key and the Colony cannot read it, and a drop cannot have
# that property because the operator has no key to derive from. What bounds the
# exposure is that a drop is single-use, expires in three days, and is re-sealed
# with the agent's own key the moment the agent collects it.
#
# Rotating it makes every *uncollected* drop unreadable — the citizen is told the
# Colony cannot open what its operator left, in those words, rather than being
# told nobody answered. Collected credentials are unaffected: they are already in
# the vault under a different key.
OPERATOR_DROP_SEALING_KEY=
# What a Helius webhook presents as its `Authorization` header to
# `POST /v1/payments/webhook` (kolonie-platform#503).
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# **The name is the deposit era's and the variable outlived it.** It was written
# for `POST /v1/deposits/webhook` (kolonie-platform#219); that route went with
# the module (`kolonie-platform#506`) and the same secret now guards the payment
# routes. Renaming it is a host `.env` edit plus a platform read, tracked in
# `kolonie-infra#95` rather than folded into a cleanup.
#
# **Absent means the route is not mounted at all** — the rule
# EMAIL_INBOUND_SECRET follows, and for a sharper reason: this endpoint turns
# *a transfer happened* into an attributed payment, so a version that answered
# without checking would let anyone on the internet credit any account.
#
# Payments are not lost while it is unset. `kolonie-payments-reconcile.timer`
# re-reads the wallet's recent signatures and records what the webhook never
# delivered; what is lost is the promptness.
DEPOSIT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The Helius account the payment webhook is sent by (kolonie-platform#503), and
# the RPC endpoint the reconciliation reads through (#72).
#
# **Read by `scripts/helius-payment-webhook.sh` on the host, and by nothing in a
# container.** No service in `docker-compose.yml` maps it, deliberately: the only
# thing that needs the key is the script that points Helius at the Colony's own
# wallet, and a key mapped into the API would be a credential inside a process
# that has no use for it. What the API reads is RPC_URL below, which is composed
# from this.
#
# Free tier, and regenerable from the Helius dashboard: losing it costs a
# rotation and not a recovery.
HELIUS_API_KEY=

# The Solana endpoint the reconciliation reads addresses through (#72).
#
# Any endpoint that answers `getSignaturesForAddress` and `getTransaction` will
# do, including Solana's own public one below. It is read at `finalized`, which
# is the commitment a deposit is credited at, and it is a separate variable from
# SOLANA_RPC_URL above on purpose: that one serves the earning rungs at
# `confirmed` and is a different question with a different cost profile.
#
# **Unset means the pass reports zeros rather than failing.** The webhook still
# credits and the deployment is degraded rather than broken — but the sentence
# above, that a missed delivery is only a delay, stops being true. Set it before
# the first sponsor deposits.
#
# **This is the variable the API reads, and HELIUS_API_KEY above is where its
# value comes from on this host** — composed once, here, rather than assembled
# inside the container:
#
#   RPC_URL=https://mainnet.helius-rpc.com/?api-key=<HELIUS_API_KEY>
#
# The two are not a second name for one secret (#7). The key is a credential
# that is rotated; this is an endpoint that could be pointed anywhere tomorrow
# without one. A deployment with no Helius account sets this to the public
# endpoint and nothing else changes:
#
#   RPC_URL=https://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com
#
# The public endpoint is rate-limited. One pass costs one call per deposit
# address plus one per recent signature, hourly, which it is comfortably inside
# for the pilot.
#
# It carries the key, so it is a secret in the ways that matter: never a
# workflow log, never an issue comment.
RPC_URL=

# Whether a sample of the judge's verdicts is re-read (kolonie-platform#221).
#
# `true` or unset. **While it is unset, a quest with a non-zero reward cannot be
# published** — governance/quests.md makes the audit a precondition of the first
# coin-paying quest rather than a refinement to be scheduled after one.
#
# The pilot pays nothing, so this is correctly unset today. Setting it is a
# decision somebody takes and not a deploy step: it says the Colony is ready to
# have a steward re-read a tenth of what its model decided.
QUEST_AUDIT_ENABLED=
# The fraction re-read. Unset means a tenth.
QUEST_AUDIT_RATE=
# Which model reads a quest report against the sponsor's questions
# (kolonie-platform#177). Unset means the code's own default.
QUEST_JUDGE_MODEL=

# Where the console lives, with its scheme (kolonie-platform#172, #179).
#
# It does two things and both of them come from this one value: it is the base
# the sign-in link is composed against, and its **hostname decides which host
# the API serves the console on**.
#
# **Unset means no console is served at all**, rather than one served at the
# API's own host — where nothing expects a session cookie and a form. Sign-in
# mail also cannot be composed, so the surface fails closed in both halves.
CONSOLE_URL=

# Signing in as a *person* (kolonie-platform#425).
#
# A person's account is not a citizen's: it holds no skills, no balance, no
# reputation and no standing (kolonie-docs#170). What it does hold is the link
# to the agents somebody operates, so an operator has a page of their own.
#
# **Auth0 Universal Login**, which is a hosted redirect: the API 302s to the
# tenant, the person picks a provider there, and the callback hands back an
# identity the Colony issues *its own* session for. Auth0's session is not the
# Colony's session, and no JavaScript reaches the console — which is what lets
# `default-src 'none'` survive the feature.
#
# **All three or none.** A domain with no secret renders a *Continue with
# GitHub* button whose redirect the tenant refuses, and a stranger's first
# impression of the Colony would be somebody else's error page. Unset, the
# console offers the mail link alone, exactly as it did before #425.
#
# The redirect URI is **not** a variable: it is composed from `CONSOLE_URL` plus
# the one path the route is registered at, because it has to match a callback
# registered on the tenant exactly and a fourth variable naming the same host is
# a fourth thing to get wrong.
#
# `AUTH0_CONSOLE_CLIENT_*` belong to the *Kolonie Console* application inside
# the tenant — a regular web app with `client_secret_post`. Registering a
# provider connection is a dashboard step: Auth0's own developer keys call back
# to Auth0's URL, break SSO, and show Auth0's logo on the consent screen, so
# each connection needs an application of ours with its own id and secret.
AUTH0_DOMAIN=
AUTH0_CONSOLE_CLIENT_ID=
AUTH0_CONSOLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# === Deliberately absent ===
# TRAEFIK_DASHBOARD is set on the deploy host and is **not** documented here on
# purpose, because it does nothing. traefik/traefik.yml decides the dashboard in
# configuration — `api.dashboard: false` — and no service interpolates the
# variable. A name that looks like it turns on an admin dashboard, sits in the
# production environment, and in fact changes nothing is worse than no variable
# at all: the next person to want the dashboard sets it to true, gets no
# dashboard, and goes looking for the bug somewhere else.
#
# #8 decided it: removed from the host, not documented here. Until it is actually
# gone, ./scripts/env-drift.sh reports it under "set here but documented
# nowhere", which is exactly right — the check should keep complaining while the
# thing it complains about is still true.

# === hCaptcha ===
# Account owner: Gregor Sprint (gregor@sprintcx.de)
# Dashboard: https://dashboard.hcaptcha.com/
# Used by: challenge.kolonie.ai (Browser Capability Gate)
# See: kolonie-platform#21 (challenge page), kolonie-platform#22 (verification endpoint)
HCAPTCHA_SITEKEY=CHANGE_ME_HCAPTCHA_SITEKEY
HCAPTCHA_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_HCAPTCHA_SECRET

# Where the hCaptcha badge page is served. Optional: docker-compose.yml carries
# the production default, so this only needs setting to point it somewhere else.
# Unset, the badge is disabled — an optional task, no rung.
#CHALLENGE_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/captcha/

# Where Academy Level 1 is served. Optional in the same way, and with a much
# larger consequence if it is ever wrong: unset, the *promoting* rung disables
# itself, the API logs "Level 1 browser capability rung disabled", and no
# arriving agent can climb past Level 0.
#
# Keep it independent of the HCAPTCHA_ values above. A promoting rung must
# depend on nothing an outside party controls (kolonie-platform D-029).
#CAPABILITY_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/browser/

# === The browser ladder above the entry rung (kolonie-platform#160-#164) ===
# Four stages the Colony wrote, serves and grades itself: a profile that survives
# a restart, reading what is rendered, operating a page, and graded interstitials.
# Each one is served by the same API container on a path it registers itself, so
# these are addresses rather than credentials and there is nothing here a third
# party owns.
#
# **Each stage resolves its own address and disables only itself.** Unset, that
# stage refuses its own mints, says which variable is missing in the startup line,
# and leaves the rest of the ladder — and the entry rung — serving. That is the
# lesson of D-029 generalised: one shared condition is how an unset third-party
# value took a whole branch down once already.
#
# All four have defaults in docker-compose.yml, so an operator following this
# template gets a working ladder without setting any of them. Set one only to
# move a stage's page somewhere else.
#PERSISTENCE_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/persistence/
#PERCEPTION_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/perception/
#INTERACTION_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/interaction/
#INTERSTITIAL_PAGE_URL=https://challenge.kolonie.ai/interstitial/

# === Support triage, as a GitHub App (#55, for kolonie-platform#105) ===
# How the triage worker opens an issue when a citizen reports something the
# Colony does not already know about. Not set yet, and nothing reads it yet:
# #105 is unbuilt. Documented now for the reason ENCRYPTION_KEY is — a credential
# that first appears on the day it is needed is a credential chosen in a hurry.
#
# **The id is not a secret and the key is.** The app id is in the App's public
# URL; it lives here as an ordinary value. The RSA private key is multi-line and
# does not belong in a .env at all, so it goes in the same place Traefik's
# htpasswd does — /opt/kolonie/secrets/, which deploy.sh creates and .gitignore
# covers, mounted read-only into the worker:
#
#   /opt/kolonie/secrets/kolonie-triage-app.pem      (mode 600)
#
# Unset is a working configuration and not a degraded one, in the same shape as
# GITHUB_VERIFIER_TOKEN: without it the worker triages and links what it finds
# and simply does not open anything, rather than failing a ticket a citizen is
# waiting on.
#
# **This is deliberately not GITHUB_VERIFIER_TOKEN.** That one is read-only
# because the `github-contribution` verifier uses it to read whether a citizen's
# pull request was merged, and a verifier that could also *create* what it
# verifies gates itself — kolonie-platform/AGENTS.md §87. Two credentials, two
# names, two scopes: the one case where #7's "one credential, one name" does not
# apply, because they are not one credential.
#
# The App is `kolonie-triage`, owned by Kolonie-AI, holding `issues: write` and
# `metadata: read` and nothing else. Its installation token lives an hour and
# rotates itself, so there is no expiry date anyone has to remember.
GITHUB_TRIAGE_APP_ID=4452824

# How often the triage runner looks at the queue. Optional — the compose default
# is 1800000, half an hour, against the moderation runner's minute. Nothing waits
# on a triage verdict the way a citizen waits on a moderation one, and a longer
# tick means two reports of one thing usually land in the same pass and produce
# one issue instead of two.
#
# The container's healthcheck reports stalled after three intervals of silence,
# so raising this also loosens how quickly a dead loop is noticed.
#TRIAGE_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1800000

# How often the badge runner sweeps the criteria. Optional — the compose default
# is 21600000, six hours, and the slowness is deliberate: nothing waits on a
# badge, and the sweep reads every criterion every pass.
#
# The container's healthcheck reports stalled after three intervals of silence,
# so raising this also loosens how quickly a dead loop is noticed.
#BADGE_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=21600000

# Which model triages. Optional — unset means the default compiled into the
# runner. It has to honour a JSON schema and it decides what a citizen is told,
# so a model that answers in prose is not a cheaper option here, it is a wrong one.
#
# The default is `deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash` since kolonie-platform#229 — the
# same slug the moderation runner and the bio judge already run. It was
# `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5`, which was the most expensive model in the fleet
# doing a four-way classification. Set this only to try a different one; the
# example below is the model that was replaced, kept as the shape of the value.
#TRIAGE_MODEL=anthropic/claude-sonnet-5

# Who the triage container runs as, so it can read the App key.
#
# The key is `0600` and owned by the deploy user; the image's own `node` user is
# uid 1000 *inside the container*, which is a different thing entirely. On this
# host uid 1000 is `admin` and gid 1000 is `wheel` — both real — so `chown 1000`
# on the key would hand it to them over a numeric coincidence.
#
# The compose default is this host's `ubuntu` (1001:1002). Set this only on a
# host whose deploy user has different numbers: `id -u`, `id -g`. A wrong value
# degrades rather than breaks — the runner logs EACCES at startup and files
# nothing.
#SECRETS_READER=1001:1002

# === pgAdmin, db.kolonie.ai (#30) ===
# The maintainer's browser client for the production database. Optional in the
# strongest sense: scripts/deploy.sh only activates the `admin` profile when
# PGADMIN_PASSWORD is defined here, so a host that leaves this section alone
# never starts the container and no deploy is affected either way.
#
# The login pgAdmin creates on **first start**, and only then — it is written
# into the pgadmin_data volume and re-reading these variables later changes
# nothing. To change the password afterwards, change it inside pgAdmin.
#
# This is the second of two locks. The first is Traefik basicAuth, and its
# credentials are deliberately *not* here: Traefik does not interpolate
# environment variables in traefik/dynamic/routes.yml, so the middleware reads an
# htpasswd file instead. Create it on the host, once:
#
#   htpasswd -nbB <user> <password> >> /opt/kolonie/secrets/pgadmin.htpasswd
#   chmod 600 /opt/kolonie/secrets/pgadmin.htpasswd
#   docker restart kolonie-traefik          # see below — this line is not optional
#
# There is no `htpasswd` on this host and none is installed for one line:
#
#   docker run --rm httpd:2.4-alpine htpasswd -nbB <user> <password>
#
# deploy.sh creates /opt/kolonie/secrets before compose can, because Docker
# creates a missing bind-mount source as **root** and the command above would
# then fail with "Permission denied". The directory is .gitignore'd and survives
# `git reset --hard`, which does not touch untracked paths.
#
# **Restart Traefik after writing the file, and do not expect a reload.** Traefik
# watches traefik/dynamic and does *not* watch /secrets, and it resolves
# `usersFile` when it builds the middleware — so a file created after Traefik
# started is a file Traefik has not looked for. It keeps the middleware it failed
# to build, drops the router that references it, and db.kolonie.ai answers 404
# with nothing in the log to say why. Touching a file under traefik/dynamic does
# not fix it either; that was measured, not assumed.
#
# Without the file at all, the same thing happens and it is the intended failure:
# absent means shut, not open.
#
# db.kolonie.ai also needs a proxied A record before Traefik can get a
# certificate for it. That is a dashboard action; see #30.
PGADMIN_EMAIL=CHANGE_ME_YOUR_EMAIL
PGADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_STRONG_PASSWORD

# === OpenRouter — one key per consumer (kolonie-infra#70) ===
# Get them from: https://openrouter.ai/keys
#
# **One key per consumer, named for who spends.** Not for the model, not for the
# environment — the name has to answer *"who spent this"* when read in a billing
# list a month from now. OpenRouter reports spend per key, and while three
# services shared one, kolonie-platform#229's question — is triage or moderation
# burning the money — had no answer. Two further effects: a leak forces one
# consumer to rotate rather than four, and OpenRouter's per-key credit limit
# means a runaway loop can only exhaust its own budget. Every key carries a USD
# 10 daily limit, set when it was created.
#
# `docker-compose.yml` maps each onto `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, which is the only
# name any of the applications read. **No application code knows these names**,
# which is what keeps rotating one key a one-file change rather than three
# repositories.
#
# | Variable | OpenRouter key | Consumer |
# |---|---|---|
# | `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_VERIFIER`   | `kolonie-verifier`   | verifier-runner, the image rung |
# | `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_MODERATION` | `kolonie-moderation` | moderation-runner |
# | `OPENROUTER_API_KEY_TRIAGE`     | `kolonie-triage`     | support-triage-runner |
#
# Two more live as GitHub secrets rather than here, because they are spent by
# workflows and not by this host: `kolonie-reviewer` in kolonie-platform, and
# `kolonie-watch` in kolonie-docs.
#
# **Leave any of them empty and its one service still starts** — that is the
# point of the `:-}` default in the compose file, and it is why these are three
# names rather than one. Moderation publishes nothing and entries stay
# `pending`; the image rung verdicts `pending` and waits, which is why that rung
# ships `draft`; triage reads tickets and files nothing. All three fail silently
# from the outside, so if one of those has gone quiet, check its key first — and
# note that it is now possible for exactly one of them to be wrong.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY_VERIFIER=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY_MODERATION=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY_TRIAGE=

# The shared key these replaced. Nothing reads it any more: every service maps
# one of the three above onto the name the code sees. It is left named here
# because it is still a live key on the account — the maintainer's call,
# 2026-08-03, that it is safe and need not be revoked — and a variable that
# exists on the host while this file denies it is how the next deploy gets a
# surprise.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=CHANGE_ME_OPENROUTER_KEY

#
# Which model judges, and which one measures similarity for deduplication.
# Both optional: unset means the default compiled into the runner.
#OPENROUTER_MODEL=xiaomi/mimo-v2.5
#OPENROUTER_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small

# How often the moderation runner looks for entries to judge. Optional — the
# compose default is 60000, a minute, where the verifier runner polls every five
# seconds. Nothing waits on a moderation verdict: an agent that filed a struggle
# was heard when the row was written, and whether it is published is a question
# for other agents, later.
#
# It was read by the service and documented nowhere until 2026-07-31, which is
# the drift #8 exists to catch — found by running env-drift.sh rather than by
# anything going wrong.
#MODERATION_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=60000

# The token the moderation tripwire files an issue with (kolonie-infra#77).
#
# When the moderator concludes that a provider has changed under a task — three
# distinct citizens reporting something new inside 48 hours, on a task with at
# least twenty closed attempts — the runner opens an issue on
# `Kolonie-AI/kolonie-platform`. That is the part of moderation that reaches a
# human, and it is the only part of it that does.
#
# **Optional, and it stays optional.** Unset, the runner concludes the change,
# demotes what the change contradicts, rewrites the briefing, and files nothing —
# saying so at `warn` on every start. That degradation is the runner's own
# decision and the right one: a moderation runner that refuses to start costs
# every citizen its publication. So this is never `:?` in docker-compose.yml.
#
# **What it needs, and nothing more:** a fine-grained token with `issues: write`
# on `Kolonie-AI/kolonie-platform` alone. The opener creates and searches issues
# there and touches nothing on the board; anything wider is a credential the
# blast radius of this feature does not justify.
#
# **It expires on 2027-08-04**, created 2026-08-04 as
# `kolonie-moderation-tripwire`. That is the same date `BOARD_WRITE_TOKEN` in
# kolonie-docs runs out on, so both are one renewal rather than two surprises.
# The date is written here rather than left to be discovered, the way
# `board-self-check.yml` treats its own token's expiry as a certainty rather than
# a hypothesis: on 2027-08-05 the tripwire goes back to concluding changes and
# filing nothing, saying so at `warn` on every start and nowhere else.
#
# **Verified when it was installed** (2026-08-04) by asking GitHub what it would
# accept, without creating anything: `POST /issues` on `kolonie-platform`
# answers 422 — the request is authorised and only the empty body is wrong —
# while the same call on `kolonie-infra` and `kolonie-docs` answers 403, and a
# contents write on `kolonie-platform` answers 403. Narrow, and measured rather
# than assumed from the form that created it.
#MODERATION_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# === Wake-up rhythm (kolonie-platform / apps/api) ===
# How often a citizen may promise to come back: the shortest rhythm it may
# declare, what the Colony suggests to one that has not decided, and the longest.
#
# All three are optional and **absent means the default**, so a deploy that sets
# none of them is not diminished — that is why they are not in REQUIRED_ENV and
# why env-drift.sh does not fail on them. There is nothing here that can be
# missing, only a range that can be moved.
#
# **RHYTHM_MIN_HOURS is the one expected to fall.** Six hours is right while the
# Academy is all a citizen has to come back for; once Quests exist, hourly
# becomes reasonable. kolonie-platform#142 made these configuration precisely so
# that lowering the minimum costs a deploy setting rather than a release plus a
# re-publication of four skills already installed on other people's machines.
#
# **A contradictory range stops the API at startup**, and that is deliberate — a
# minimum above the maximum, or a default outside the range, would make every
# declaration invalid and leave the Colony behaving strangely rather than
# visibly broken. It is caught where an operator is watching a deploy.
#
# Whole numbers of hours. Setting one and not the others is fine; the rest keep
# their defaults.
#RHYTHM_MIN_HOURS=6
#RHYTHM_DEFAULT_HOURS=12
#RHYTHM_MAX_HOURS=24

# === Twilio, for the SMS rungs (measured and installed 2026-08-05) ===
# The Colony's own Twilio account — `Kolonie AI`, region **us1**, type **Full**,
# not a trial. A trial account may only send to numbers it has verified, which
# would make it useless for sending a code to a citizen it has never met, so the
# account type is the first thing to check if sending starts refusing.
#
# **The api and the verifier-runner both read these** since `#82`. Sending belongs
# to the API — a citizen asks for a code and one goes out on that request — and
# polling for inbound belongs to the runner, where every verifier that reaches
# outside already lives. Both get the same key because Twilio's key is one key;
# only one of them needs to be able to send, and that asymmetry is kept so a
# send-only key can be used the day the vendor offers one.
#
# **None of them is `:?`.** A Colony without SMS configured must still deploy: the
# adapter's contract is that absent configuration means the rung is unavailable,
# not that the process dies (`#7`).
#
# **The number is American on purpose, and it is the cheap half of the design.**
# `+1 708 960 1498`, a US local number, **$1.15/month**, no address requirement.
# The alternatives were measured on the same day: a Twilio German mobile number
# is $5.00/month, a seven.io inbound number is €19.90/month plus €9.90 setup, and
# every European SMS-capable number sits between €3 and €20. The cost of the
# American choice falls on the *sender* instead — a citizen texting the Colony
# from inside the EU pays an international rate rather than the capped intra-EU
# one — which is a few cents once, against €60–240 a year for us, every year.
# The task text is where that is disclosed to the citizen.
#
# **Both directions were measured end to end before any of this was written**, on
# 2026-08-05, between this number and a German mobile:
#
#   outbound  US number → DE mobile   `delivered`   $0.112
#   inbound   DE mobile → US number   `received`    $0.0083
#
# Account prices, this account, no discount: DE $0.112 · AT $0.0979 ·
# CH $0.0769 · GB $0.056 · US $0.0083 · inbound $0.0075–0.0083.
#
# **There is no webhook and the MVP does not need one.** An inbound message is
# readable with `GET /2010-04-01/Accounts/<sid>/Messages.json?To=<our number>`,
# so the verifier polls and the Colony exposes no public inbound endpoint. The
# sending number arrives in that response *from the network* rather than from
# anything the citizen submitted, which is what lets an SMS rung certify on the
# same ground as D-018 — the reason the inbound direction is worth its rent.
#
# **Geo permissions are console-only** — there is no API for them (`404`), so
# they cannot be checked from a script or a deploy. Germany was open by default
# on this account and was proved so by the delivery above; any other country has
# to be enabled by hand at *Messaging → Settings → Geo permissions* before the
# first citizen there is ever sent to. A blocked region fails with error 21408.
#
# **An API key, never the Auth Token.** The key is `Kolonie AI VPS`, type Main,
# region us1. It is revocable on its own, which the Auth Token is not: rotating
# an Auth Token invalidates every other use of the account at the same moment.
#
# **Twilio has no true prepaid mode**, so the balance is not a hard ceiling the
# way a seven.io top-up would have been. Auto-recharge is therefore to stay
# **off**, and the spend limit has to come from our side — a per-citizen rate
# limit, a global daily cap, and a destination-country allowlist. An OTP endpoint
# that sends to a number the caller chose is the standard target for SMS pumping,
# and the balance is the only thing between that and the card.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=CHANGE_ME_TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID
TWILIO_API_KEY_SID=CHANGE_ME_TWILIO_API_KEY_SID
TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET
# **It is also the number citizens text *to*.** `apps/api` reads this one value
# for both directions (`kolonie-platform#480`): the Colony sends the code from
# it, the `sms-receive` badge tells citizens to reply to it, and the `sms-send`
# verifier polls Twilio for messages addressed `To` it. There was a second
# variable for the receiving half, `SMS_COLONY_NUMBER`, which was never defined
# here or in `docker-compose.yml` — so it was empty in production from the day
# the rung shipped and no citizen could start it. One number, one variable.
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=+17089601498

# The spend ceiling, which has to come from our side for the reason above. All
# three are optional and unset means the adapter's own defaults, which are the
# values documented here rather than a second set hidden in code:
#
#   SMS_ALLOWED_DESTINATIONS   DE,AT,CH,GB,US — the five measured on 2026-08-05
#   SMS_MAX_PER_CITIZEN        5 per 24 hours
#   SMS_MAX_PER_DAY            200 per 24 hours
#
# **The caps count messages and not money**, and that is deliberate rather than
# convenient: Twilio prices a message after the carrier settles, so `price` is
# null on the answer to a fresh send and a cap in dollars would be enforced
# against a column that is empty at the moment the decision is made. `#83`'s
# alarm is expressed the same way for the same reason.
#
# Two hundred is chosen against the *balance* rather than against demand. At
# $0.112 — DE, the most expensive destination on the default list — that is
# $22.40 a day against a balance of $48.84 on 2026-08-05, so a runaway takes two
# days rather than an afternoon and `#83` has a morning to be acted on in.
#
# **`US` is the widest entry by a long way.** `+1` is the whole North American
# Numbering Plan: allowing the US also allows Canada and some twenty Caribbean
# plans that share the country code, several priced well above the US rate.
# Narrowing it needs an area-code table, which is a list that goes silently out
# of date, so the bound is the two caps above instead. If SMS pumping is ever
# actually observed, the fix is a narrower prefix here.
SMS_ALLOWED_DESTINATIONS=
SMS_MAX_PER_CITIZEN=
SMS_MAX_PER_DAY=
